        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: fateor, fatērī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fateor, fatērī — \"to admit, to confess\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tandem fūr fassus est sē crumēnam rapuisse. At last the thief admitted that he had snatched the purse."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fateor, fatērī, fassus sum, a deponent, with accusative and infinitive. English confess comes from a compound of it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "fateor is inherited from Proto-Italic fatēōr (EtymDB), usually traced to a root meaning \"to speak\", also seen in fārī, \"to speak\". English confess comes from a compound."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
